l computers that can generate a lot of heat, especially when gaming or fast-charging. Glass is completely unaffected by these temperatures. Plastic, on the other hand, can warp, distort, or degrade when subjected to constant heating and cooling cycles. **5. Oleophobic Coatings** Phone screens are treated with an "oleophobic" (oil-repelling) coating so your fingerprints easily wipe off. These chemical coatings bond incredibly well to glass, but they do not bond nearly as well—or last as long—on plastic surfaces. ### How engineers compromise: Chemically Strengthened Glass Because consumers hate shattered screens but also hate scratched screens, companies like Corning created **Gorilla Glass** (and Apple uses a similar concept called **Ceramic Shield**). These are not standard pieces of glass. They are bathed in molten salts to force larger ions into the surface of the glass, creating a state of constant compression. This chemical engineering makes the glass much more flexible and shatter-resistant than normal glass, while maintaining the scratch-resistance and clarity of glass. ### The Exception: Foldable Phones Your idea of using flexible plastics is actually being used right now in **foldable phones** (like the Samsung Galaxy Z Fold and Z Flip). Because glass cannot fold in half without breaking, these phones *must* use plastic (or a microscopic layer of ultra-thin glass covered by plastic). However, these foldable screens perfectly demonstrate the downsides of plastic: they scratch very easily (even a hard press with a fingernail can leave a permanent dent), they have a visible crease, and they don't feel quite as smooth as a standard glass phone.
